[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Decomposition and Pattern Recognition in Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Computational thinking relies heavily on two foundational pillars: decomposition and pattern recognition. When faced with complex architectural challenges or intricate algorithmic requirements, tackling the entire problem at once often leads to failure or inefficient solutions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Decomposition"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Decomposition is the process of breaking down a complex problem or system into smaller, manageable, and independent parts. Each sub-problem can then be examined, designed, and tested in isolation before integration."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identify the primary system objective or core problem."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolate functional boundaries and separate concerns."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Break down large modules into granular functions or micro-tasks."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying Pattern Recognition"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a problem is decomposed, pattern recognition allows us to search for similarities, trends, and shared characteristics among the sub-problems. Recognizing these patterns enables the reuse of existing solutions, design patterns, and optimized algorithms."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function processUserData(user) {
    // Decomposed validation step
    if (!validateField(user.email, 'email')) return false;
    if (!validateField(user.age, 'number')) return false;

    // Pattern recognition: shared transformation logic
    return sanitizeInput(user);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging decomposition and pattern recognition, software engineers can write scalable, maintainable, and robust code. Always analyze your problem space thoroughly before writing the first line of code."}]
